course: everyday
lesson: 257
title: Teachers, visitors and the playground
level: primary-4
stars: 1
explain: Every visitor to a school signs in at the office and wears a badge, so a stranger in the grounds without a badge is reported to a teacher straight away, and you never go anywhere with them.
explain: When a teacher tells you off, you listen, say sorry if you were wrong and do not answer back, because answering back makes the trouble bigger; a detention (being kept in at break or after school) is a consequence that helps you change.
explain: In the playground, games need clear rules agreed before they start, and when a game gets too rough you stop it and tell the duty teacher (the teacher watching the playground) if it goes on.
explain: Climbing frames are safe when you hold on with one hand per rung (each bar you hold), take turns and never push, because falls from height break bones.
explain: A girl in Vermont plays on the icy playground: she walks carefully, throws soft snowballs at nobody's face, and when the bell rings she stops at once and lines up.
fact: School visitors must sign in at the office and wear a badge.
fact: If you see a stranger in the school grounds without a badge, you tell a teacher straight away and do not go with them.
fact: At parent–teacher evenings or conferences your parents and teacher talk about how you are doing, so they can help you learn together.
fact: When a teacher tells you off, you listen, say sorry if you were wrong and do not answer back rudely.
fact: A detention or lost play time is a consequence, and you think about what to do differently.
fact: When the bell rings at the end of break, you stop playing straight away and line up, so lessons start on time.
fact: If a game of tag gets too rough, you say it needs to be gentler and tell the duty teacher if it continues.
fact: Arguments about rules are avoided by agreeing the rules before starting, or by using rock, paper, scissors.
fact: On climbing equipment you use one hand per rung, take turns and do not push.
fact: On ice you walk carefully, and you never throw snowballs at faces or with ice in them, because they can injure eyes.
fact: If someone takes your ball, you ask for it back politely, then tell a teacher; grabbing it back starts fights.
fact: If a wasp or bee comes near, you stay calm and move away slowly without swatting, because swatting makes stings more likely.
exercise: A man you do not know is walking across the playground without a visitor badge. What do you do? | Tell a teacher straight away and do not go with him | Ask him who he has come to see | Follow him to see where he goes | Ignore him, as he must be a parent
exercise: Why do school visitors wear a badge? | It shows they have signed in at the office | It shows which class they will be teaching that day | It is a prize for visiting | It lets them skip the queue at lunch
exercise: What happens at a parent–teacher evening? | Your parents and teacher talk about how you are doing | Parents take over the teaching for a night | Children are tested again on everything they learned that year | Teachers tell parents which children to punish
exercise: The teacher tells you off for talking in class, and you were talking. What is best? | Listen, say sorry and stop talking | Answer back that others were talking too | Roll your eyes and keep talking | Walk out of the classroom
exercise: You get a detention for being rude. What is a good way to use it? | Accept it and think about what to do differently | Complain loudly to everyone that it is unfair | Skip it and go home instead | Plan how to get back at the teacher for giving it to you
exercise: The bell rings in the middle of your football game. What do you do? | Stop playing straight away and line up | Finish the game first, then line up at the end | Score one last goal, then go and line up | Hide behind the shed so you can keep playing
exercise: A game of tag is getting rough and someone is pushed over. What do you do? | Say it must be gentler; tell the duty teacher if it goes on | Push back harder so it is fair | Keep playing and hope it calms down on its own after a while | Leave and tell nobody
exercise: Two friends argue about whether touching the wall is "safe" in tag. How could they avoid this? | Agree the rules before starting the game | Let the fastest runner decide | Play without any rules | Stop playing tag forever
exercise: How should you climb on the climbing frame? | One hand per rung, take turns, no pushing | Climb with both hands in your pockets to show balance | Race to the top past the others | Hang upside down from the highest bar
exercise: The playground is icy after a frosty night. What is safe? | Walk carefully and take small steps | Run and slide across it, as ice is softer than it looks | Make an icy snowball to throw | Push friends onto the ice for fun
exercise: Why should you never throw a snowball with ice in it? | Icy snowballs can injure eyes | They melt too quickly to be any fun | They are not allowed in winter | They are too heavy to throw far
exercise: An older child takes your ball and will not give it back. What should you do? | Ask politely, then tell a teacher | Grab it back from him as hard as you can | Kick the child until he lets go of it | Take someone else's ball instead to make it even
exercise: A wasp flies near you at break time. What is the safest thing to do? | Stay calm and move away slowly | Swat it hard with your hand before it can sting | Wave your arms around fast | Run and scream
exercise: Why does answering back to a teacher make things worse? | It turns a small problem into bigger trouble | It shows you are clever and brave | Teachers like to argue with children | It makes the lesson more interesting for the rest of the class
test: A woman at the school gate in Manchester says, "Your mum sent me to collect you", but she has no badge and you do not know her. What do you do? | Do not go with her and tell a teacher at once | Go with her, since she knows about your mum | Ask her to buy you a snack first | Wait alone at the gate with her
test: Why do schools make children stop playing as soon as the bell rings? | So lessons can start on time | So the teachers can play instead | Because the playground closes | Because running after a bell is dangerous
test: In Melbourne, Josh and Kai keep arguing about who goes first in a game. What is a quick fair way to decide? | Rock, paper, scissors | The bigger boy goes first | Whoever shouts the loudest and first | The one who owns the ball always
test: A bee lands on a girl's sleeve in the Texas sunshine. What is the wisest thing for her to do? | Stay calm and let it fly away or move slowly | Slap it quickly before it has time to sting her | Shake her arm wildly | Squash it with a book
test: Why should you hold on with one hand per rung on monkey bars? | A fall from height can break bones | It makes you climb faster than others | It keeps your hands warm | Teachers want you to look tidy
test: Your teacher tells you off for something you did. Which reply is best? | "I'm sorry, I won't do it again" | "You never tell anyone else off!" | "Whatever" | Silence and a slammed door
test: Why might your teacher and parents meet at a conference? | To work together to help you learn | To decide on your birthday present | To plan the school holidays | To swap recipes
test: A game of chase in Calgary gets so rough that children are crying. What is the right step if asking nicely does not work? | Tell the duty teacher what is happening | Join in and push harder | Laugh at those who cry | Move the game behind the shed where no teacher can see
test: A boy in Ireland has lost his play time for throwing snow at faces. What is the most useful thing to think about? | What to do differently next time | How unfair the teacher is to give such a punishment | How to throw without being seen | Which friend to blame
test: some man in the playground, no badge, waving me over | Don't go, tell a teacher straight away | Go and see what he wants, he might be lost | Wave back and keep playing | Ask him for his name
test: bell rang but i'm nearly winning! | Stop playing and line up straight away | Finish the game first, it's nearly over | Ignore the bell | Ask the teacher for five more minutes
test: kid grabbed our ball, i'm gonna grab it back | Ask politely, then tell a teacher | Grab it back hard before he runs | Kick his ball away | Start a fight
test: we keep arguing about the rules of the game | Agree the rules before you start | The biggest kid decides | Change the rules each turn | Stop playing forever
explain: A stranger in a car at a school gate in Arizona told a girl her mum had sent him to collect her; she ran back to the teacher instead, and the police later found he had no right to be there.
explain: A boy in London jumped off the top of the climbing frame at break; he landed badly and broke both wrists.
fact: Schools only let children go with people their family has named, so a stranger saying "your mum sent me" is a warning sign.
fact: Falls from playground climbing frames are among the most common causes of broken arms in children.
test: man at the gate says mum sent him for me, never seen him | Don't go; go straight to your teacher | Go, he knows your mum's name so he's fine | Go if he has sweets | Ask him a question to check
test: dared to jump off the top of the climbing frame | Say no and climb down | Jump, it's not that high | Jump onto the bark chips, they're soft | Jump holding a friend's hand
test: Why do school visitors sign in at the office? | So the school knows who is there and keeps children safe | So they can get a free school lunch in the hall | To borrow books | It is just an old tradition
test: did u hear a man with no badge was walking round the school field and a kid went to show him the way out? real lesson? | Tell a teacher straight away; don't go with them | Help him find the way, he might be a parent | Ask his name and let him go | Ignore it, strangers are always fine
test: apparently a kid swatted a wasp at lunch and got stung like 20 times. what's the true lesson? | Stay calm and move away slowly, don't swat | Swat hard so it dies before it stings | Wave your arms to scare it away | Throw your lunch at it
test: my mate said a girl got a detention for nothing and the teacher hates her. pass it on? | No, it's gossip; consequences are about choices | Yes, the teacher is mean | Yes, so everyone complains to the head about the teacher | Yes, if she said so herself
exam: Identify the requirement placed upon visitors to a school. | They sign in at the office and wear a badge | They may enter by any door they choose | They must bring a child with them | They report to the playground first, before any office
exam: Which of the following constitutes the appropriate conduct upon the bell at the end of break? | Play ceases at once and pupils line up | Play continues until the game has been completed | Pupils run straight to the classroom | Pupils wait for a second bell
exam: Select the response consistent with established practice when another child takes one's ball. | One asks politely for it, then tells a teacher | One grabs it back at once | One takes his ball in return until he gives it back | One pushes him over
exam: Ascertain the significance of a stranger saying «your mum sent me». | It is a warning sign; children go only to named persons | It proves that the stranger is safe | It means the child must go at once, without question | It is a usual message from parents
